WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.308
Middleware order affects whether rate limiting applies as intended.

2
00:00:04.308 --> 00:00:05.593
In ASP.

3
00:00:05.593 --> 00:00:13.373
NET Core, routing, authentication, authorization, rate limiting, and endpoint mapping all contribute to the request path.

4
00:00:13.373 --> 00:00:21.988
Endpoint-specific rate-limiting APIs need the pipeline to identify endpoint metadata before the limiter decides which policy to apply.

5
00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:29.831
The relationship between routing, authentication, authorization, rate limiting, and endpoint mapping should be deliberate.

6
00:00:29.831 --> 00:00:32.660
Some policies may need endpoint information.

7
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.939
Some partition keys may use authenticated identity.

8
00:00:35.939 --> 00:00:40.440
Some endpoints may require authorization before expensive work begins.

9
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:48.540
Teams should understand which context the limiter uses and whether that context exists at the point where the middleware runs.

10
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.912
Global limits and endpoint-specific limits serve different purposes.

11
00:00:52.912 --> 00:00:55.998
A global limit can provide a broad safety floor.

12
00:00:55.998 --> 00:01:01.013
Endpoint-specific policies can reflect the actual cost and purpose of a route.

13
00:01:01.013 --> 00:01:10.014
A report endpoint, upload endpoint, login endpoint, search endpoint, and health check probably should not all be treated as equivalent work.

14
00:01:10.014 --> 00:01:12.586
Endpoint scope should be easy to review.

15
00:01:12.586 --> 00:01:20.043
Minimal API groups, controller attributes, route conventions, and named policies can make rate-limit intent visible.

16
00:01:20.043 --> 00:01:28.337
Reviewers should be able to tell which endpoints are limited, which policy applies, and why a given endpoint differs from others.

17
00:01:28.337 --> 00:01:33.288
Disabling or overriding rate limits should be rare, documented, and reviewed.

18
00:01:33.288 --> 00:01:44.603
There may be legitimate operational endpoints or internal workflows that need special behavior, but exceptions should have an owner, reason, environment scope, and review date.

19
00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:48.589
An exception without ownership becomes a long-term blind spot.
